  "headline": "Where the Buddha first taught",
  "short_description": "A Buddhist heritage site about 10 km from Varanasi, with the Dhamek Stupa, monastery ruins and the museum that holds the Ashoka Lion Capital.",
  "about": "Sarnath is a compact archaeological and pilgrimage site north-east of Varanasi. It is associated in Buddhist tradition with the Buddha's first sermon and is a common half-day trip from Varanasi.\n\n\n\n\n\n",
  "history": "- **c. 3rd century BCE — Ashokan pillar and lion capital at Sarnath.** A Mauryan-era pillar surmounted by a lion capital stood at Sarnath; the capital is now in the Sarnath Archaeological Museum and is the basis of India's national emblem. Marks Sarnath's importance in the Mauryan period.\n- **c. 5th–6th century CE — Dhamek Stupa built in its present form.** The Dhamek Stupa is generally dated to the Gupta period, on the site of earlier structures.",
  "history_verified": false,
